\subsection{Computer-Assisted Analytical Closure of the Live Certificate}
\label{sec:iss-computer-assisted-certificate}

The Phase-2 certificate separates the logical stability implication from the
construction of uniform constants.  The eight-sea diagnostic demonstrates
strong realized Riccati-metric contraction, but it is not part of the theorem.
This subsection closes the missing analytical steps by verified arithmetic over
a declared compact domain.  The proof program
\texttt{tools/ou\_live\_basin\_interval\_proof.py} contains no sea spectrum,
random seed, replay trajectory, or fitted stability margin.

\subsubsection{Certificate domain and source contract}

The fixed dimensionless scales are those of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-scales}.  The source/default part of the proof domain
uses the default tuning path $\tau_\star=(2f_{\rm tune})^{-1}$ with
$f_{\rm tune}\le\SI{1.5}{Hz}$ and the source ceiling
$\tau\le\SI{12}{s}$, so
\begin{equation}
 \frac13\ {\rm s}\le\tau\le12\ {\rm s}.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-tau-domain}
\end{equation}
The wrapper also applies a source-enforced OU standard-deviation floor before
committing the stationary covariance,
\begin{equation}
 \sigma_{aw,i}\ge\SI{0.05}{m/s^2},
 \qquad
 \sigma_{aw,i}\le\SI{6}{m/s^2},
 \label{eq:iss-ca-sigma-domain}
\end{equation}
for all three default axes because $S_{\rm factor}=1$.  This is stronger than
the tuner output alone: the wrapper evaluates
\texttt{max(0.05, band\_noise\_floor, sigma\_applied)} before calling
\texttt{set\_aw\_stationary\_std}.  Hence a positive OU diffusion floor is an
implementation property of the default wrapper and is not introduced merely to
make the proof work.

The remaining source/default quantities used below are the finite
$\tau_b=\SI{5000}{s}$ residual accelerometer-bias model, the validated sensor
noise configuration, and the audited effective $S$-pseudo standard-deviation
enclosure
\begin{equation}
 0.044\le r_S\le694\ {\rm m\,s}.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-rs-domain}
\end{equation}
The proof tool checks these source tokens and aborts if they drift.

The assumptions not enforced by the current source remain explicit certificate
conditions:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
\SI{4.5}{ms}\le h_k\le\SI{5.5}{ms},\\
5\le\|\widehat f_b\|\le15\ {\rm m/s^2},\qquad
10\le\|\widehat m_b\|\le100\ {\rm \mu T},\\
1-|u_f^\top u_m|\ge0.05,\qquad
\|\widehat\omega\|\le\SI{1.5}{rad/s},\\
\SI{5}{ms}\le\Delta\le\SI{50}{ms}
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:iss-ca-external-domain}
\end{equation}
for the selected accepted vector-update pair.  These conditions delimit the
certified set; they are not inferred from covariance or from simulation.

\subsubsection{Exact Riccati energy identity}

Let $P_k$ denote the scaled covariance and let
\begin{equation}
 V_k(\vct z)=\vct z^\top P_k^{-1}\vct z.
\end{equation}
For one accepted measurement with prior covariance $P^-$,
innovation covariance $S=C P^-C^\top+R$, and gain
$K=P^-C^\top S^{-1}$, the homogeneous correction is
$z^+=(I-KC)z^-$.  Using the exact covariance update gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{V^- -V^+
 = (Cz^-)^\top S^{-1}(Cz^-).}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-meas-energy}
\end{equation}
A covariance-consistent MEKF reset is a congruence and therefore leaves $V$
unchanged.  Prediction is nonexpansive because
$P^-=F P F^\top+Q\succeq FPF^\top$.
Telescoping over one horizon gives the exact identity
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 P_0^{-1}-\Psi_H^\top P_H^{-1}\Psi_H=\mathcal G_H\succeq0,}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-energy-identity}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal G_H$ is the accumulated prediction and measurement dissipation.
If $P_0=L_0L_0^\top$ and $P_H=L_HL_H^\top$, then any verified
\begin{equation}
 L_0^\top\mathcal G_HL_0\succeq\underline\delta_H I
 \label{eq:iss-ca-energy-margin}
\end{equation}
immediately implies
\begin{equation}
 \|L_H^{-1}\Psi_HL_0\|_2
 \le\sqrt{1-\underline\delta_H}<1.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-energy-contraction}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Finite-horizon process-noise posterior}

Stack the whitened process noise over one horizon into $\xi$ and write
\begin{equation}
 z_H=\Phi_Hz_0+\mathcal B_H\xi,
 \qquad
 Y=\mathcal O_Hz_0+\mathcal H_H\xi+v.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-batch}
\end{equation}
Giving an auxiliary estimator the exact initial state can only reduce its error
covariance.  Conditional on that oracle information, the minimum covariance of
the terminal process-noise contribution is
\begin{equation}
 P_{\rm or}=\mathcal B_H
 (I+\mathcal H_H^\top R^{-1}\mathcal H_H)^{-1}
 \mathcal B_H^\top.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-oracle}
\end{equation}
Therefore the noise-only covariance of the actual Kalman estimator satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_H\succeq P_{\rm or}.
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal B_H\mathcal B_H^\top\succeq\alpha_c I,
 \qquad
 \|\mathcal H_H^\top R^{-1}\mathcal H_H\|\le\beta_c,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\Omega_H\succeq q_HI,\qquad
 q_H=\frac{\alpha_c}{1+\beta_c}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-qh}
\end{equation}

The remaining question is to obtain $\alpha_c$ without reverting to the tiny
one-sample $Q_{SS}=O(h^7)$ eigenvalue.  This can be done for an
\emph{arbitrarily time-varying} OU pole; no adaptation-slew assumption is
needed.

For one scaled OU--III axis write
\begin{align}
 \dot z_v&=\frac{s_{aw}}{s_v}z_a,&
 \dot z_p&=\frac{s_v}{s_p}z_v,\\
 \dot z_S&=\frac{s_p}{s_S}z_p,&
 \dot z_a&=-\lambda(t)z_a+\frac{\sqrt{q_c(t)}}{s_{aw}}u,
 \label{eq:iss-ca-scaled-axis}
\end{align}
where
$q_c(t)=2\sigma_{aw}^2(t)/\tau(t)$ and
$\lambda(t)=1/\tau(t)$.  The source bounds give
$q_c(t)\ge q_{c,-}=2(0.05)^2/12$ and
$0<\lambda(t)\le3$.

Fix the horizon length $T_H$ and any desired terminal scaled state
$z_f=(z_v,z_p,z_S,z_a)$.  Choose the acceleration trajectory
\begin{equation}
 z_a(t)=\sum_{j=1}^4 c_j(t/T_H)^j.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-steering-poly}
\end{equation}
It starts from $z_a(0)=0$.  Triple integration and the endpoint condition give
$z_f=M_Hc$, where
\begin{equation}
M_H=
\begin{bmatrix}
A_1/2&A_1/3&A_1/4&A_1/5\\
A_2/6&A_2/12&A_2/20&A_2/30\\
A_3/24&A_3/60&A_3/120&A_3/210\\
1&1&1&1
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
with
$A_1=s_{aw}T_H/s_v$,
$A_2=s_{aw}T_H^2/s_p$, and
$A_3=s_{aw}T_H^3/s_S$.
The normalized rational part has the exact inverse
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
60&-360&840&-4\\
-420&2340&-5040&30\\
780&-4080&8400&-60\\
-420&2100&-4200&35
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
so
\begin{equation}
 \|c\|^2\le\Gamma_M(T_H)\|z_f\|^2,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma_M=
 \frac{964800}{A_1^2}+
 \frac{26661600}{A_2^2}+
 \frac{114307200}{A_3^2}+5741.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-moment-inverse}
\end{equation}

The control that realizes Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-steering-poly} is
$u=s_{aw}(\dot z_a+\lambda z_a)/\sqrt{q_c}$.  The derivative and value Gramian
row-sum bounds are respectively $241/35$ and $19/20$; hence
\begin{equation}
\int_0^{T_H}u^2dt
\le
\frac{2s_{aw}^2}{q_{c,-}}
\left[
 \frac{241}{35T_H}+\frac{19}{20}\lambda_+^2T_H
\right]
\Gamma_M(T_H)\|z_f\|^2.
\label{eq:iss-ca-steering-energy}
\end{equation}
The minimum control energy for reaching $z_f$ is
$z_f^\top W_c^{-1}z_f$.  Therefore Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-steering-energy}
proves the uniform controllability inequality
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 W_c(T_H)\succeq\alpha_c I,\qquad
 \alpha_c=\Gamma_c^{-1},}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-controllability}
\end{equation}
for every measurable/piecewise-constant schedule in the declared box.  Because
the implementation uses the exact OU finite-step covariance for each
piecewise-constant sample, this continuous finite-horizon Gramian is exactly the
accumulated discrete process covariance of the model.

For the maximum proof horizon
$T_H=\SI{0.5074090909}{s}$, verified arithmetic gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\alpha_c\ge6.881415950832\times10^{-19}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-alpha-num}
\end{equation}
The older final-sample bound is retained only as a diagnostic:
\begin{equation}
 q_{\rm step}\ge2.591300053522\times10^{-33}.
\end{equation}
Thus the finite-horizon steering argument removes approximately
$2.66\times10^{14}$ of artificial process-floor conservatism while allowing
arbitrary time variation of $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ inside the certificate
box.  A frozen-schedule calculation gives
$2.955831842793\times10^{-20}$ and is therefore weaker than
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-alpha-num}; it is no longer needed even as a strengthening.

\subsubsection{Constructive observability and covariance upper bound}

For one translational axis, four $S$ observations have
\begin{equation}
 S(t)=S_0+t p_0+\frac12t^2v_0+A_3(t)a_0.
\end{equation}
The third divided difference annihilates the quadratic polynomial and isolates
$a_0$.  For successive gaps at least $T_-$,
\begin{equation}
 \|w_3\|_2\le\frac{\sqrt5}{3T_-^3},
\end{equation}
and Hermite--Genocchi gives
\begin{equation}
 A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3]
 \ge\frac16e^{-3T_+/\underline\tau}.
\end{equation}
After recovering $a_0$, ordinary second and first divided differences recover
$v_0,p_0$, while the first sample recovers $S_0$.  Carrying the state scaling
and the complete effective-$r_S$ enclosure through this explicit inverse yields
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_L)
 \ge1.216353822821\times10^{-15}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-sl}
\end{equation}
This is the expected $T_-^{-3}$ inverse scaling rather than the much more
conservative determinant/Frobenius $T_-^{-6}$ construction.

The two-vector attitude/gyro-bias construction gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_A)
 \ge3.161536500037\times10^{-5}.}
\end{equation}
Combining these through the actual block-triangular 18-state observation matrix
and bounding the acceleration-to-vector cross block gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_{18})
 \ge6.707390504261\times10^{-23}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-s18}
\end{equation}

An explicit finite-window comparison observer for these 18 coordinates, with
the stable residual accelerometer bias treated as bounded colored nuisance,
then gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{P_H\preceq\overline pI,\qquad
 \overline p\le4.176061086161\times10^{57}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-pbar-num}
\end{equation}
The bound is intentionally a worst-case theorem constant, not a realistic
covariance estimate.

The process-to-selected-measurement operator satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \beta_c\le1.052749938580\times10^{10},
\end{equation}
so Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-qh} gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{q_H\ge6.536610165432\times10^{-29}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-qh-num}
\end{equation}
Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-qh-num} and
\eqref{eq:iss-ca-pbar-num} with
$P_H=\Psi_HP_0\Psi_H^\top+\Omega_H$ yields
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\delta_H
 \ge1.565257315582\times10^{-86}>0,}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-delta-num}
\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{1-\chi_H
 \ge7.826286577912\times10^{-87}>0.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-chi-margin}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Independent observable/detectable energy closure}

The covariance-decomposition proof above already establishes strict
contraction.  We additionally verify the structural detectable decomposition
suggested by Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-energy-identity} rather than relying on a raw
$21\times21$ interval eigenproblem.

Let $x\in\mathbb R^{18}$ collect the uniformly observable coordinates and let
$b\in\mathbb R^3$ denote the residual accelerometer bias.  The selected
measurement-energy term can be bounded in the form
\begin{equation}
 c_m\|\mathcal O_{18}x+D_bb\|^2,
\end{equation}
where $c_m>0$ accounts for innovation covariance and the strictly lower
triangular correction-feedback map.  The script uses
$\|KR^{1/2}\|\le\sqrt{\overline p}/2$ and the finite-horizon transition bound
to obtain a verified $c_m$.

For the $b_a$ OU prediction, Woodbury gives a process-dissipation lower bound on
the bias subspace even with arbitrary covariance cross terms.  If
$q_b$ is the scaled exact one-step residual-bias covariance, then
\begin{equation}
 d_b\ge
 \frac{q_b}{\overline p^2(1+q_b/\overline p)}>0.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-db}
\end{equation}
With
$\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_{18})\ge s_{18}$, define
\begin{equation}
 L_*=\begin{bmatrix}
 1/(\sqrt{c_m}s_{18})&
 \|D_b\|/(s_{18}\sqrt{d_b})\\
 0&1/\sqrt{d_b}
 \end{bmatrix}.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-detect-left-inverse}
\end{equation}
Then the block operator satisfies
\begin{equation}
 c_m\|\mathcal O_{18}x+D_bb\|^2+d_b\|b\|^2
 \ge\frac{1}{\|L_*\|_2^2}\|(x,b)\|^2.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-detect-bound}
\end{equation}
The verified broad-box value is strictly positive but more conservative than
the covariance-decomposition route:
\begin{equation}
 \delta_{\rm det}
 \ge4.988195497798\times10^{-203}>0.
\end{equation}
Its role is therefore structural: it confirms independently that measurement
dissipation covers the observable 18-state block while the finite residual-bias
OU process closes the remaining detectable direction.

\subsubsection{Lifted nonlinear Riccati-metric basin}

The remaining nonlinear constants can also be bounded without replay.  From
$P\preceq\overline pI$ and $R\succeq\underline rI$,
\begin{equation}
 \|K_z\|\le\frac{\overline p\,\|C_z\|}{\underline r}.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-gain-bound}
\end{equation}
Substitution of this inequality into the accelerometer/magnetometer Taylor
remainders and the $\SO(3)$ composition bound of
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-cert-remainder} yields the verified one-step constant
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{c_\xi\le2.603125914091\times10^{126},}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-cxi-num}
\end{equation}
with chart radius
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{r_\xi\ge3.348105435485\times10^{-63}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-rxi-num}
\end{equation}
These values are intentionally worst-cased independently and are not engineering
tuning numbers.

At horizon boundaries define
\begin{equation}
 R_n=\|P_{k_n}^{-1/2}z_{k_n}\|.
\end{equation}
Every intermediate linear Riccati step is nonexpansive.  If
$p_{-,i}$ is a verified intermediate covariance floor, then the nonlinear
injection satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \|\varphi_i\|_{V_{i+1}}
 \le c_\xi\frac{\overline p}{\sqrt{p_{-,i}}}R_i^2.
\end{equation}
Summing these coefficients over the at most 113 samples in one proof horizon
defines $C_H$, and the lifted recursion is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 R_{n+1}\le\chi_HR_n+C_HR_n^2+G_H\overline d.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-lifted-recursion}
\end{equation}
For zero deterministic disturbance, a sufficient invariant radius is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 R_{\rm cert}
 <\min\left\{
 \frac{r_\xi}{\sqrt{\overline p}},
 \frac{1-\chi_H}{C_H}
 \right\}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-lifted-radius}
\end{equation}
The verified broad-box calculation gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{R_{\rm cert}
 \ge3.243203027309\times10^{-289}}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-lifted-radius-num}
\end{equation}
as a strictly positive sufficient radius.  This is mathematically constructive
but operationally useless.  For nonzero bounded disturbance the usual quadratic
discriminant condition
$(1-\chi_H)^2>4C_HG_H\overline d$ gives the corresponding practical invariant
tube.

\subsubsection{Findings and scope}

The computer-assisted closure changes the interpretation of the Phase-2
certificate in four ways.

First, the one-sample triple-integrator floor is no longer the theorem's process
bottleneck.  The arbitrary-time-varying finite-horizon steering construction
improves the verified controllability floor by approximately
$2.66\times10^{14}$ while requiring no schedule-freezing or slew hypothesis.

Second, the translational observability constant is constructed by divided
differences rather than by a determinant/Frobenius conversion, yielding the
correct $T_-^{-3}$ inverse scaling.

Third, the full 21-state detectable structure is closed analytically through the
observable 18-state block and the finite residual-bias OU direction; a raw
interval eigenvalue sweep is unnecessary.

Fourth, the nonlinear Riccati-metric radius is now computed rather than left as
an uninstantiated formula.  Its value shows that the remaining obstacle is not
strict stability but conservatism of the global covariance/measurement bounds,
especially the complete effective-$r_S$ enclosure and the block-triangular
cross coupling.  The present broad-domain proof is therefore a valid existence
certificate, not a useful numerical handoff test.

No reference-sea simulation is needed for any implication in this subsection.
The existing \texttt{live\_basin\_diagnostic} remains useful only for measuring
how conservative these analytical constants are relative to realized operation.
No filter or tuner behavior is changed by this proof.
